i said i wasn't going to post another  video about sunscreen but  hi it's michelle from lab muffin beauty  science chemistry phd and skincare nerd  and serial sunscreen video maker in case  you haven't already heard the 2021  version of this anti-cancer product is  actually giving you cancer  is that there's been benzene found in a  bunch of sunscreens  how much should we be freaking out about  this i did an instagram post on this  already  but i thought i'd elaborate a bit more  in a video  like subscribe notification bell you  know the drill first let's talk about  the results before we go into some of  the more  questionable things about this report  it's from valley shaw a us-based online  pharmacy that brands themselves as the  pharmacy that checks the premise is the  fda doesn't regulate drugs as closely as  valley shore would like so  valley shore test them before they sell  them so valley shore bought a bunch of  products and sent them to some labs for  testing using gas chromatography  it was sunscreen and after sun care  products from 294 batches from 69  different brands they found benzene at  low pass per million concentrations in  78 of these  most of them were spray sunscreens there  was over 2 parts per million in 14  different batches between 0.1 and 2  parts per million and 26  and the rest were below 0.1 parts per  million for reference two parts per  million is one one fiftieth of a drop in  a product this big  and one three hundredth of a drop in a  product this big they pick two parts per  million because that's the limit that  the fda recommends for  products like hand sanitizers and  medications the table in attachment a  also has a list of sunscreens where they  didn't detect any benzene  i've seen a lot of stuff online about  this that just isn't quite right so  let's clear up a few things  firstly no one is purposely adding  benzene to products benzene doesn't  really have a purpose in cosmetics it's  not a fragrance  it doesn't absorb uv strongly enough to  be used as a sunscreen  it's not a preservative it has no  beneficial function that can't be done  more safely and cheaply with a different  ingredient  benzene is a trace contaminant so it's  in there because it was an impurity  in another ingredient or something  happened during the manufacturing  process  it's also not an issue that's specific  to sunscreens there was benzene found in  after sun care products in that highest  group and  this was an issue that was in hand  sanitizers in an earlier report  it's not because of particular sunscreen  ingredients or chemical sunscreens  both mineral and chemical sunscreens  show up on the list valley shul also  checked raw sunscreen ingredients for  benzene and they didn't find any  there are some sunscreen ingredients  that sort of look like the word benzene  but  they don't have anything to do with  benzene sort of like how lady gaga  doesn't have much to do with galileo  it also isn't a problem with particular  brands all of the brands tested that had  high benzene also had products with no  benzene  except for the one brand that only had  one product tested it's also not  specific to particular products  all of the products where they tested  more than one batch had different levels  of benzene so it seems like sometimes  when the brand made the product there  was more benzene  and then sometimes when they made it  there was less so if you want to find  out if your product has benzene in it  you don't just match the name of the  product you also want to match  the other parts of the table the upc lot  and expiry date  there are also 4 lists so make sure  you're looking at the right list  if it says nd that means they didn't  detect benzene  but how worried should we actually be  about benzene  there are two phrases in the report that  i think were misinterpreted and they  make benzene sound a lot scarier than it  actually is  benzene is a known human carcinogen and  there's no safe level of benzene  and it kind of sounds like anyone who's  used one of these sunscreens has  upped their chances of cancer by a lot  but that's not really true  known human carcinogen is a  classification from the iarc  these classifications known probable and  possible human carcinogen tell us about  how certain the evidence is that  something  increases the chance of cancer it  doesn't tell us how big  that increase actually is so some things  that are really potent are in the same  category as things that are a lot weaker  for example we know that walking near a  dog definitely increases your risk of  getting bitten by a dog  so does punching a dog so they're both  known dog bite causes  but obviously one of these is much worse  we can see this in action by looking at  some of the other known carcinogens so  smoking cigarettes and eating processed  meat i think we all know there's a  massive difference  in the risk from smoking cigarettes  versus eating a few slices of salami  and on top of that i think we all know  there's a big difference between smoking  one cigarette when you were 16  and smoking 30 cigarettes a day for 50  years you might remember from my video  about clean beauty the idea of hazards  versus risks  these classifications are hazard-based  if you haven't watched that check it out  it really puts the idea of toxic  chemicals in your beauty products into  perspective  the other phrase that i think got taken  out of context is that there's  probably no safe level of benzene  combined with known human carcinogen i  think it really makes it sound like any  exposure to benzene  is dangerous but if you look at the rest  of the sentence in this toxicology  context  safe means absolutely zero effect  no safe level means that little  exposures can add up  but the question is how much is the  benzene in sunscreen adding to the  amount of benzene we're already getting  a few of us pointed out on instagram  that we're actually exposed to quite a  lot of benzene in our everyday lives  so we need to think about that to  contextualize how much extra we're  getting from the benzene in sunscreens  we mostly get benzene by inhaling it so  for example in petrol or gasoline if  you're american  there's one to three percent benzene so  we can compare it more easily that's ten  to thirty thousand parts per million  cigarette smoke and bushfires also have  benzene also indoor air  there's tiny bits of benzene that's left  over in manufacturing plastics and stuff  that get released into the air over time  we also eat it in food and it's in  drinking water but these amounts that we  get in everyday life  aren't enough to lead to cancer the link  to cancer is with much higher amounts so  usually with industrial exposure where  workers are working with benzene every  day  for most of us we inhale 3 to 40  micrograms per cubic meter of air  and this is about a thousand times lower  than the amount that's been found to be  problematic and linked to cancer  now for skin contact like we'd get with  sunscreens unfortunately there's a lot  less information  that's because the main concern with  benzene is industrial exposure and most  industrial exposure is from inhaling it  benzene is volatile which means most of  it actually evaporates before it has a  chance to get through your skin  into your body how much gets through  your skin really depends on what the  benzene is in  but in general it's a fraction of a  percent of the amount that you apply  and not all of the stuff that gets  through the skin actually makes it to  the rest of your body  one paper estimated that a worker who  puts their hands and lower half of their  forearms in one thousand parts per  million benzene for one hour a day  and their skin is damaged so it's going  to absorb more than normal  then over 40 years they'd have a  relative risk of leukemia of 1.42  so that means they're one and a half  times more likely to develop leukemia  compared to someone who didn't have this  exposure to benzene but  if this was a hundred parts per million  instead then there would be close to no  increased risk of leukemia so 100 parts  per million was the limit recommended  for workers  in sunscreens the highest amount was six  parts per million so  based on the evidence it doesn't really  seem like six parts per million is going  to make that much of a difference  joe schwartz chemistry professor and  professional debunker thinks that  there's an infinitesimal chance that the  benzene in sunscreen will lead to any  significant amount in blood  don't get me wrong benzene is definitely  harmful there's no benefit to it i'm not  recommending that you slather yourself  on purpose with lots of benzene we  should be reducing our exposure to  things like benzene and it's a good  thing that regulators are working on it  but like with other harmful and  hazardous things  our risk depends on our exposure the  harm depends on how much you get and how  you're exposed to it  and this aspect gets lost a lot of the  time when we're talking about scary  things on our beauty products i've  talked about this in the context of  dirty lists from clean beauty companies  but this sort of scary story has been  happening for a long time before clean  beauty for example there was a big thing  about lead and lipstick in like the late  90s early 2000s  there are trace amounts of lead and  lipstick and there's nothing beneficial  about lead  but the amounts and lipstick are so tiny  that they're not going to have any  real effect on your health there are a  few things in the valley shore report  that i think  are a bit confusing so for example the  dermatologist that they quoted as an  expert  says there is not a safe level of  benzene that can exist in sunscreen  products and even benzene at 0.1 parts  per million  in sunscreen could expose people to  excessively high nanogram amounts of  benzene  i think this doesn't really reflect what  we know about the toxicology of benzene  for example in the eu benzene is allowed  in some cosmetic ingredients as an  impurity if it's below 1000 parts per  million  to make it below 0.1 plus per million  these ingredients would have to be used  at less than 100 of a percent  and these ingredients are used at much  higher concentrations than that  the excessively high nanogram amounts of  benzene bit is also really weird because  we're normally exposed to much higher  amounts that we measure in micrograms  not nanograms  a 2010 paper estimated that non-smokers  inhale about 200 to 450 micrograms  or 200 000 to 450 000 nanograms of  benzene a day  and again this amount is really tiny  compared to the industrial exposures  that were actually linked to cancer  there's also a really scientifically er  comparison in the report there's another  not very nice substance called  ndma not mdma that's been found in some  pills  and the fda has limited that to two  parts per million as well  this translates to 96 nanograms a day so  they use this as a comparable benchmark  to the amount of benzene you get from  sunscreen if you apply four shot glasses  with two parts per million  this comparison is really whack for a  number of reasons so  obviously we apply a lot more sunscreen  than a tablet-sized amount  so if they were allowed at the same  concentration then benzene is obviously  a lot safer  like if you compare one caffeine tablet  to four shot glasses of caffeine tablets  the thing you can throw back four shots  of is obviously going to be a lot safer  and of course it's also a completely  different route of exposure we swallow  tablets we don't swallow sunscreen  well i'm not going to assume i know what  you do with your life but  it's a silly comparison there's also the  disposal recommendations so valley sure  recommend that you dispose of the  benzene sunscreens as hazardous waste  but as this environmental scientist who  works with hazardous waste points out it  is a really tiny amount of benzene 1  300th of a drop in a tube this size  hazardous waste people are probably not  going to know what to say if you ask  them about it  the fact that sunscreen sprays are  flammable is probably a bigger safety  issue  again i think benzene is harmful and i'm  not saying that you shouldn't reduce  your exposure to it but hopefully this  gives you some context for how worried  you should be  if you've been using one of these  sunscreens or if you're using a  sunscreen that hasn't been tested  also it's good to remember that benzene  in personal care products has never been  linked to cancer  but uv exposure has i would personally  probably stop using  products from the batches that were  measured to have higher amounts i would  probably still hold on to them though in  case we get more information or if  there's a recall  will the fda actually issue a recall  well i think joe schwartz is right it's  not really scientifically justified but  the government does a lot of things that  aren't scientifically justified  save sunscreens check out my video on  clean beauty if you haven't already i  also have tons of other videos about  sunscreens  thanks to steven for helping me with  this video also wasabi.jiff for her  insights check them both out on  instagram if you haven't already  next video is definitely not sunscreen

